# ================================================================================
# Cubicle Management System - Frontend Git Exclusion Configuration
# ================================================================================
#
# @fileoverview Git version control exclusion patterns for the Cubicle Management
# System frontend Vue.js application. Optimizes repository performance, enhances
# security, and prevents unnecessary files from being tracked in version control.
#
# @version 2.0.0
# @author IBM Space Optimization Developer - Wander Jimenez Calvo
# @since 1.0.0
#
# Purpose:
# - Exclude build artifacts and generated files from version control
# - Prevent sensitive configuration and environment files from being committed
# - Reduce repository size and improve clone/fetch performance
# - Maintain clean development workflow and prevent conflicts
# - Optimize Docker build context and CI/CD pipeline efficiency
#
# Repository Impact:
# - Typical size reduction: 80-95% of potential repository bloat
# - Clone time improvement: 60-80% faster repository operations
# - Security enhancement: Sensitive files and credentials excluded
# - Build optimization: Cleaner Docker build contexts
#
# Pattern Syntax:
# - Use Unix-style glob patterns for cross-platform compatibility
# - Comments start with # for documentation and organization
# - Negation with ! to include files that would otherwise be excluded
# - Directory patterns end with / to match directories specifically
# - Use ** for recursive directory matching
#
# Maintenance:
# - Review patterns quarterly for relevance and effectiveness
# - Add new patterns when introducing new tools or dependencies
# - Test exclusions with 'git check-ignore' command
# - Document rationale for complex or non-obvious patterns
#
# ================================================================================

# ================================================================================
# DEPENDENCIES AND PACKAGE MANAGEMENT
# ================================================================================
#
# Node.js dependency directories and package manager artifacts
# These directories contain downloaded packages and should be regenerated
# from package.json/package-lock.json rather than tracked in version control
#
# Impact: Prevents massive dependency trees from bloating repository
# Security: Avoids potential security issues from committed node_modules
# Performance: Significantly reduces repository size and operation times
#

# Node.js dependency directory - contains all npm/yarn installed packages
# Should be regenerated via 'npm install' or 'yarn install'
node_modules/

# Package manager lock files (keep these for dependency consistency)
# Note: These files ARE tracked to ensure consistent dependency versions
# !package-lock.json
# !yarn.lock
# !pnpm-lock.yaml

# Package manager cache directories
.npm/
.yarn/
.pnpm/

# ================================================================================
# BUILD ARTIFACTS AND GENERATED FILES
# ================================================================================
#
# Files and directories created during the build process
# These are generated from source code and should not be version controlled
# Can be recreated using 'npm run build' or similar build commands
#
# Impact: Prevents build artifacts from causing merge conflicts
# Performance: Reduces repository size and improves build consistency
# Security: Avoids exposing compiled code with embedded configurations
#

# Vite build output directory - contains optimized production assets
# Generated by 'npm run build' and contains minified, optimized code
dist/

# Server-side rendering build output (if using SSR)
dist-ssr/

# Development build cache and temporary files
.vite/
.cache/
.temp/
.tmp/

# TypeScript build output and cache
*.tsbuildinfo
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# ================================================================================
# DEVELOPMENT AND DEBUGGING FILES
# ================================================================================
#
# Files generated during development, debugging, and testing processes
# These files are specific to individual developer environments
# Should not be shared between team members or deployed to production
#
# Impact: Prevents development artifacts from cluttering repository
# Security: Avoids exposing debugging information and local configurations
# Performance: Keeps repository focused on source code only
#

# Debug log files from various tools and package managers
# Generated during development, debugging, and error scenarios
logs/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
lerna-debug.log*

# Runtime data and temporary files
pids/
*.pid
*.seed
*.pid.lock

# Coverage directory used by tools like istanbul and nyc
# Generated by test coverage tools and should be recreated on demand
coverage/
*.lcov
.nyc_output/

# ================================================================================
# ENVIRONMENT AND CONFIGURATION FILES
# ================================================================================
#
# Environment-specific configuration files containing sensitive information
# These files often contain API keys, database credentials, and other secrets
# Should be managed per environment and never committed to version control
#
# Security: Critical for preventing credential exposure and security breaches
# Flexibility: Allows different configurations for different environments
# Best Practice: Use environment variables and .env.example templates instead
#

# Environment variable files containing sensitive configuration
# Use .env.example or .env.template for documenting required variables
.env
.env.local
.env.development.local
.env.test.local
.env.production.local

# Local configuration overrides
config.local.js
config.local.json

# Firebase configuration files (if containing sensitive data)
# Note: Public Firebase config can be tracked, but private keys should not be
firebase-debug.log
.firebase/

# ================================================================================
# EDITOR AND IDE SPECIFIC FILES
# ================================================================================
#
# Files and directories created by various editors and IDEs
# These are specific to individual developer preferences and environments
# Should not be imposed on other team members who may use different tools
#
# Impact: Prevents editor conflicts and maintains tool flexibility
# Collaboration: Allows team members to use their preferred development tools
# Performance: Keeps repository clean of tool-specific metadata
#

# Visual Studio Code configuration (partial exclusion)
# Keep extensions.json for shared team extensions, exclude personal settings
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/settings.json.example

# JetBrains IDEs (WebStorm, IntelliJ IDEA, etc.)
.idea/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws

# Sublime Text
*.sublime-workspace
*.sublime-project

# Vim and Neovim
*.swp
*.swo
*.swn
*~

# Emacs
*~
\#*\#
.\#*

# ================================================================================
# OPERATING SYSTEM SPECIFIC FILES
# ================================================================================
#
# Files automatically created by various operating systems
# These files provide OS-specific functionality but are not part of the project
# Should be excluded to maintain cross-platform compatibility
#
# Impact: Prevents OS-specific files from causing issues on other platforms
# Collaboration: Ensures clean repository regardless of developer OS
# Performance: Reduces repository size and prevents unnecessary file tracking
#

# macOS system files
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
ehthumbs.db
Thumbs.db

# Windows system files
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# Linux system files
*~
.fuse_hidden*
.directory
.Trash-*

# ================================================================================
# TESTING AND QUALITY ASSURANCE
# ================================================================================
#
# Files generated by testing frameworks, quality assurance tools, and automation
# These files are typically generated during CI/CD processes or local testing
# Should be regenerated on demand rather than tracked in version control
#
# Impact: Prevents test artifacts from cluttering repository
# Performance: Keeps repository focused on source code and configuration
# Automation: Allows CI/CD systems to generate fresh test results
#

# Test output and reports
test-results/
test-reports/
junit.xml
coverage/

# Playwright test results and artifacts
test-results/
playwright-report/
playwright/.cache/

# Jest and other testing framework cache
.jest/
jest-coverage/

# ESLint cache files
.eslintcache

# Prettier cache
.prettiercache

# Stylelint cache
.stylelintcache

# ================================================================================
# DEPLOYMENT AND PRODUCTION FILES
# ================================================================================
#
# Files related to deployment processes and production configurations
# These files may contain sensitive deployment information or be environment-specific
# Should be managed through proper deployment pipelines and configuration management
#
# Security: Prevents exposure of deployment credentials and production configs
# Best Practice: Use proper deployment tools and environment management
# Flexibility: Allows different deployment strategies per environment
#

# Deployment artifacts and configuration
deploy/
deployment/
.deploy/

# Docker development overrides (keep main docker files)
docker-compose.override.yml
.dockerignore.local

# Kubernetes development configurations
k8s-dev/
*.dev.yaml

# Static site generators and deployment
.vercel/
.netlify/
.surge/

# ================================================================================
# PERFORMANCE AND MONITORING
# ================================================================================
#
# Files generated by performance monitoring, profiling, and analysis tools
# These files are typically large and specific to local development or testing
# Should be generated on demand for performance analysis
#
# Impact: Prevents large performance files from bloating repository
# Performance: Keeps repository size manageable
# Analysis: Allows fresh performance profiling for each analysis session
#

# Performance monitoring and profiling
.clinic/
profile/
*.prof
*.cpuprofile
*.heapprofile
*.heapsnapshot

# Bundle analysis reports
bundle-report.html
stats.json

# Lighthouse reports
lighthouse-report.html
lighthouse/

# ================================================================================
# TEMPORARY AND BACKUP FILES
# ================================================================================
#
# Various temporary files, backups, and autosaves created by different tools
# These files are typically transient and should not be tracked permanently
# Can be safely excluded as they don't contain unique project information
#
# Impact: Prevents temporary files from causing repository bloat
# Maintenance: Reduces need for manual cleanup of temporary files
# Stability: Prevents conflicts from temporary file differences
#

# General temporary files
*.tmp
*.temp
*.bak
*.backup
*.orig

# Editor autosaves and temporary files
*.sw?
*.suo
*.ntvs*
*.njsproj
*.sln

# File synchronization
.sync/
.syncthing/

# Archive files (typically temporary for development)
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*.rar
*.7z

# ================================================================================
# CUSTOM PROJECT EXCLUSIONS
# ================================================================================
#
# Project-specific exclusions that don't fit into standard categories
# Add custom patterns here for specific project requirements
# Document rationale for non-obvious exclusions
#

# Project-specific documentation builds (if using separate build process)
# docs/build/
# docs/.vuepress/dist/

# Project-specific tools and utilities
# tools/temp/
# scripts/output/

# Example: Large assets that should be managed via Git LFS instead
# assets/videos/*.mp4
# assets/large-images/*.psd

# ================================================================================
# END OF CONFIGURATION
